ta lag, a second round-robin matchup, or document inconsistency that materially reduces confidence in predictive strength."}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.68, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "DK.C's 2-0 win in Round 1 is real but occurred ~1-2 weeks ago; meta shifts in LoL are rapid and BRO.C's 3-0 over Gen.G in Week 3 suggests tactical evolution. BRO.C's win record is distorted by early-season losses; recent games show 3-0 demolition (GEN), indicating non-linear improvement trajectory that briefing aggregates mask. Briefing explicitly notes 'early season performance may not be fully representative' \u2014 this is a data freshness blind spot; BRO.C could have pivoted mid-split while market assumes stale Week 1-2 form. Roster/coaching changes are plausible but unconfirmed in briefing; no player injury or substitution data provided for either team, leaving hidden personnel risk. DK.C's organizational pedigree (Dplus = 2020 Worlds winner affiliation) may create complacency or underestimation of BRO.C in a rematch, especially if BRO.C has tangible recent evidence of improvement. Market price (70% YES) reflects aggregate early-season stats but does not price in the Week 3 BRO.C 3-0 with appropriate recency weight; this is a classic value trap in rapidly-evolving esports. Confidence in NO side is low (45) because briefing lacks: BRO.C's detailed roster, recent patch-specific draft analysis, head-to-head meta matchup data, and coaching/player form comments \u2014 significant asymmetry favors conservative NO confidence. DK.C's confidence remains moderately high (72) due to hard 2-0 evidence, organizational stability, and multiple 2-0 wins in Week 2, but recent BRO.C 3-0 introduces material doubt. This is a TODAY event (9 hours to resolution); any lineup surprise, server issue, or pre-match roster announcement would lock in low shares immediately \u2014 currently market trades at fair value but contains hidden tail risk.", "ke