-0 record with a +4 round differential and 3 points, while 100 Thieves has a 0-0-1-1 record with a -5 round differential and 1 point. Both teams are competing in Group A of the tournament, which began on April 1, 2026.", "side_yes_case": ["M80 leads in head-to-head recent form with 1-0-0-0 record vs 100 Thieves' 0-0-1-1 record", "M80 has superior round differential (+4) compared to 100 Thieves (-5)", "M80 currently holds 3 points in standings vs 100 Thieves' 1 point", "100 Thieves has lost multiple matches (0 wins, 2 losses), indicating inconsistent performance", "M80 appears to have stronger early tournament momentum"], "side_no_case": ["100 Thieves' record shows 1 win against weaker opposition in the round differential breakdown", "Both teams are still early in tournament play with limited match data", "100 Thieves has shown adaptability with recent performance improvements in later playdates", "M80's 1-0 record may not reflect true competitive depth in a single BO1 match", "Tournament format volatility in early stages can favor underdog performances"], "key_context": ["Match is Best of 1 format, increasing variance and upset potential", "Both teams are in early Kickoff stage (Playday 4) with limited sample size for assessment", "Tournament features 10 teams across North America competing online on PC", "No direct head-to-head historical data provided between M80 and 100 Thieves in this tournament", "Map pool includes 9 maps: Bank, Border, Chalet, Clubhouse, Consulate, Kafe Dostoyevsky, Lair, Nighthaven Labs, Fortress"], "data_freshness": "2026-04-09T22:16:49.370905Z"}}, "resolution": {"resolved_at": "2026-04-10T05:00:00Z", "winning_side": "YES", "final_yes_price": 1.0, "checked_at": "2026-04-10T09:42:07.333677+00:00", "source": "gamma_api"}, "analysis": null}
{"run_id": "b947ed12-8a97-4a0d-906d-4cbe29c2f007", "timestamp": "2026-04-09T22:17:09.043399+00:00", "legacy": false, "result": {"market": {"id": "1870723", "question": "Rainbow Six Siege: Cloud9 vs Outlast (BO1) - North America League Kickoff Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Rainbow Six Siege match between Cloud9 and Outlast in the North America League Kickoff Group B, initially scheduled for April 9 at 7:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Cloud9\" if Cloud9 win the match against Outlast.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Outlast\" if Outlast win the match against Cloud9.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://liquipedia.net/rainbowsix/Main_Page. However, if https://liquipedia.net/rainbowsix/Main_Page has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-10T06:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T00:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.755, "volume_usd": 7.901228, "category": "rainbow-six-siege"}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.54, "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.41, "rationale": "CONDITION TO RESOLVE: Cloud9 must win the BO1 match against Outlast scheduled for April 9, 7:00 PM ET to resolve YES. STATISTICAL CASE FOR CLOUD9 (slight edge): Cloud9 shows -9 round differential vs Outlast's -11 (better by 2 rounds); this is the only quantifiable differentiator in briefing data. Both teams are 0-2 with 0 points, making records identical. STATISTICAL CASE FOR OUTLAST (near parity): Despite -11 RD, Outlast and Cloud9 have identical win-loss records (0-2, 0p). The 2-round differential is marginal in a BO1 context where single-map outcomes are binary. BO1 FORMAT HIGH VARIANCE: Best-of-One matches in tactical shooters like R6 Siege are inherently high-variance. A 2-round differential in round counts does not translate to reliable win probability prediction in a single map. BO1 eliminates series momentum and map selection strategy advantages. MOTIVATION AND CONTEXT: Both teams are 0-2 and winless. Cloud9 may have marginal infrastructure/experience edge (noted in CASE FOR YES), but briefing provides no concrete evidence of this. Outlast as underdog has equal desperation motivation. INSUFFICIENT HEAD-TO-HEAD DATA: Briefing contains no direct H2H history, recent streaks, or opponent-specific performance data. The round differentials are aggregate across multiple opponents (DarkZero, Spacestation Gaming), not indicative of Cloud9 vs Outlast specific strengths. MARKET PRICING SIGNAL: Current market YES = 76% / NO = 24% implies strong Cloud9 favoritism. This may reflect external information (team rosters, player skill ratings, recent scrims) NOT present in the briefing. Briefing data alone supports only ~54% Cloud9 edge. TIMING RISK (SECONDARY): Event timing is TODAY, ~8 hours from briefing. Match is imminent and likely already underway or about to begin. If match is canceled, delayed >7 days, or ends in forfeit/walkover, market resolves 50-50 per criteria. Probability of disruption is low but non-zero. RESOLUTION SOURCE: Liquipedia is reliable source. No ambiguity in team name matching (Cloud9 and Outlast are clearly identified in standings table). Oracle delay risk is low given 2-hour fallback to credible reporting. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY JUSTIFICATION: Both confidence_yes and confidence_no are set low (42/41) because briefing data is sparse on predictive factors. The 2-round RD edge for Cloud9 is real but modest and insufficient to justify high confidence on either side in BO1 context. High external market pricing (76%) suggests missing data favoring Cloud9, which reduces confidence_no further but does not raise confidence_yes given data scarcity. FINAL PROBABILITY: 54% reflects a modest Cloud9 edge based on -9 vs -11 RD, adjusted downward from market price (76%) due to BO1 variance and lack of head-to-head evidence. This is a near coin-flip with slight YES lean.", "key_factor": "Insufficient comparative data on Cloud9 vs Outlast head-to-head performance and recent form trends; BO1 format creates high variance that undermines predictive confidence regardless of slight statistical edge."}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.76, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.48, "rationale": "FAVORED SIDE STRENGTHS (Cloud9/YES): Cloud9 holds a +2 round differential advantage over Outlast (-9 vs -11), indicating slightly more competitive play and closer losses against DarkZero and Spacestation Gaming. In a BO1 format where single rounds matter, this marginal edge in competitiveness is meaningful. UNDERDOG WEAKNESSES (Outlast/NO): Outlast has been outscored 3-14 across matches, a -11 differential suggesting more fundamental execution problems. The briefing notes Outlast shows 'more fundamental issues' compared to Cloud9's competitive rounds, indicating deeper tactical or skill gaps. MARKET PRICING RATIONALE: 76% YES reflects the round differential advantage and competitive context. The market is pricing this as a slight favorite for Cloud9, not overwhelming\u2014appropriate given both teams are equally winless (0-2, 0p) and in direct desperation mode. BO1 VARIANCE CAVEAT: Best-of-One formats inherently favor upsets and increase variance. However, marginal statistical edges (round differential, competitive indicators) still persist across variance. Cloud9's better differential suggests it wins the coin-flip matchups slightly more often. BOTH TEAMS' MOTIVATIONAL STATE: Both teams are 0-2 and seeking their first win\u2014motivation is symmetric. The briefing correctly notes this is 'critical opportunity' for either team, but doesn't indicate Outlast has superior morale or momentum. No contrarian catalyst for Outlast is evidenced. INFRASTRUCTURE/EXPERIENCE ARGUMENT: Briefing suggests Cloud9 'may have edge in team infrastructure and experience,' though not heavily detailed. This is consistent with Cloud9 being historically a more established NA org, which could translate to composure in BO1 elimination-like scenarios. COMPETITIVE ENVIRONMENT NEUTRALITY: Both teams faced same opponents (DarkZero, Spacestation). Cloud9 performed slightly better in round counts despite losses, demonstrating relative edge in the same competitive environment. DATA FRESHNESS & TIMING: Data is from 2026-04-09 22:16 UTC; match is TODAY (resolves ~8 hours away, 2026-04-10 06:00 UTC). No recent roster changes, roster crises, or live updates in briefing suggest the round differential and record standings remain current assessment basis. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: YES confidence is 72 because Cloud9's marginal structural advantages (RD, competitive rounds) are documented and consistent. NO confidence is only 48 because Outlast's case relies on motivation symmetry and BO1 variance\u2014neither are strongly evidenced in briefing. The underdog story is narratively plausible but data-light. IS 76% CORRECT PRICING?: The market appears appropriately calibrated. Cloud9's +2 RD edge in a BO1 context justifies moderate favorite status (65\u201380 range). At 76%, YES is slightly above baseline but not overpriced given the structural indicators. Market is neither a steal nor a trap.", "key_factor": "Cloud9's superior round differential (-9 vs -11) and marginally better competitive performance against top teams despite matching record suggests structural advantage in a high-variance BO1 format."}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.38, "rationale": "RECORD PARITY: Both Cloud9 and Outlast are 0-2 with 0 points. This is the strongest indicator of match balance. ROUND DIFFERENTIAL EDGE: Cloud9 is -9 (5-14 rounds); Outlast is -11 (3-14 rounds). Cloud9's marginally better RD (+2) is the only quantifiable performance gap, but both are severely underperforming. BO1 VARIANCE: Single-map format creates high upset potential and reduces predictability compared to BO3/BO5. Base rate for 'favored' team in BO1 esports is ~55-60%, not 76%. MARKET PRICE DIVERGENCE: YES is trading at 76%, implying ~3:1 confidence in Cloud9. This substantially exceeds the narrow empirical gap (Cloud9's -9 vs Outlast's -11 RD) and suggests potential overconfidence in Cloud9. NO DATA ON TEAM-SPECIFIC FACTORS: Briefing lacks head-to-head history, roster quality, recent scrims, coaching changes, or map pool preferences. Claims of 'Cloud9 infrastructure advantage' are unsubstantiated by provided data. BOTH TEAMS DESPERATE: Equal motivation (first win needed) cancels out any psychological edge. Underdog status is symmetric in a 0-2 vs 0-2 matchup. SYMMETRY OF STRUGGLE: Both teams scored only 3-5 rounds against DarkZero/Spacestation. No evidence Cloud9 showed notably 'more competitive' performance in losses. BASE RATE ADJUSTMENT: For true parity (0-2, 0p, minimal RD gap, BO1 format), base rate should be 50-52%. Market price of 76% for YES assumes a decisive skill gap not clearly evidenced in the data provided. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: High confidence in YES requires strong differentiation (it's minor). High confidence in NO requires data on Outlast's upside (lacking). Both sides suffer from limited information.", "key_factor": "In BO1 esports matches between evenly-matched teams with similar recent records, the higher-seeded or slightly-favored team wins ~55-60% of the time; in true coin-flip scenarios (identical records, similar round differentials), outcomes approach 50%."}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.62, "confidence_no": 0.58, "rationale": "Market priced YES at 76% reflects a slight Cloud9 edge, but briefing data shows both teams are nearly identical: both 0-2, both 0 points, only -2 round differential gap (Cloud9 -9 vs Outlast -11). The 76% YES price appears to overweight Cloud9's marginal advantage. Round differential of -9 vs -11 is a weak signal in BO1 format where variance is high. Briefing explicitly notes both teams are 'evenly matched in overall record' and 'competing for first win' \u2014 suggests ~50-55% territory, not 76%. Crowd may be anchoring on 'Cloud9 infrastructure/experience' narrative without strong data. Both teams are equally helpless (0-2 in same league, same opponents). BO1 format dramatically increases upset potential. Cloud9's slight round differential edge should compress probability in single-game format. Market exhibits mild Greed bias toward the YES side: sentiment overstates a marginal statistical edge (2 round differential) into 3:1 odds. No recent head-to-head data provided. Infrastructure claims in briefing are speculative, not factual. Confidence in NO is asymmetric because briefing provides no unique Outlast strengths to justify the gap \u2014 market may be underestimating underdog motivation and BO1 variance. At 76% YES, market is pricing in high confidence Cloud9 wins. Actual data gap (2-round diff, identical records) does not justify this conviction. Rational adjustment: slight Cloud9 edge exists but should yield ~55-60% probability, not 76%. This suggests YES is moderately overpriced; NO offers modest value.", "key_factor": "Rational"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.62, "confidence_no": 0.48, "rationale": "Both teams are 0-2 with identical record standings (0 points), indicating comparable performance level in identical tournament structure and opponent pools. Cloud9's -9 RD vs Outlast's -11 RD is marginal; in BO1 format, a 2-round differential across multiple maps is not predictive of head-to-head outcome. BO1 format inherently high-variance in tactical shooters: single map pool, one-game elimination of team synergy issues\u2014favors hot team or one executing fundamentals cleanly, not historical record. Both teams showing severe round losses (Cloud9 5-14, Outlast 3-14) suggests both have fundamental execution/communication breakdowns; neither demonstrates tactical superiority warranting 76% confidence. Market pricing at 76% YES appears overweighted on Cloud9's marginally better RD and brand perception; actual competitive gap likely smaller. No match history, recent team changes, or meta-knowledge provided in briefing; cannot assess roster competency, recent practice results, or coaching adjustments. Motivation/psychological factors favor Outlast as desperate underdog; Cloud9 may face pressure from slight favoritism. DarkZero (3-0) and SSG (2-1) leading group shows tournament is competitive; both 0-2 teams are operating well below group average, increasing uncertainty. Event timing: match is LIVE/IMMINENT (~8 hours to start); major reversal unlikely but variance in BO1 tactical esports means share prices will swing sharply post-match regardless. Confidence split justified: YES side has marginal statistical edge (RD), but NO side lacks sufficient counter-evidence due to data parity and BO1 volatility.", "key_factor": "In BO1 tactical shooters, round differential matters less than team composure under pressure\u2014both teams' 0-2 records suggest systemic issues (communication, strategy execution, or player confidence) rather than raw skill gaps, making outcome highly volatile and closer to 50-50 than market prices suggest."}], "final_score": 0.6202857142857143, "no_score": 0.38995708154506437, "edge": -0.13471428571428568, "no_edge": 0.14495708154506437, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "YES-side confidence too low: 56% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1870723", "question": "Rainbow Six Siege: Cloud9 vs Outlast (BO1) - North America League Kickoff Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Rainbow Six Siege match between Cloud9 and Outlast in the North America League Kickoff Group B, initially scheduled for April 9 at 7:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Cloud9\" if Cloud9 win the match against Outlast.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Outlast\" if Outlast win the match against Cloud9.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://liquipedia.net/rainbowsix/Main_Page. However, if https://liquipedia.net/rainbowsix/Main_Page has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-10T06:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T00:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.755, "volume_usd": 7.901228, "category": "rainbow-six-siege"}, "key_facts": ["| # | Team | Record | RND | RD | Pts |\n| 1.", "| WildcardWildcard Wildcard | 1-0-0-0 | 7-1 | +6 | 3p |\n| 2.", "| DarkZeroDarkZero DarkZero | 1-0-0-0 | 7-4 | +3 | 3p |\n| Cloud9Cloud9 Cloud9 | 0-0-0-0 | 0-0 | 0 | 0p |\n| 4.", "| Spacestation GamingSpacestation Gaming Spacestation Gaming | 0-0-0-1 | 4-7 | -3 | 0p |\n| 5.", "| OutlastOutlast Outlast | 0-0-0-1 | 1-7 | -6 | 0p |\n| 1.", "| DarkZeroDarkZero DarkZero\u25b21 | 2-0-0-0 | 14-8 | +6 | 6p |\n| 2.", "| WildcardWildcard Wildcard\u25bc1 | 1-0-0-1 | 11-8 | +3 | 3p |\n| 3.", "| Spacestation GamingSpacestation Gaming Spacestation Gaming\u25b21 | 1-0-0-1 | 11-11 | 0 | 3p |\n| 4.", "| Cloud9Cloud9 Cloud9 | 0-0-0-1 | 4-7 | -3 | 0p |\n| 5.", "| DarkZeroDarkZero DarkZero | 3-0-0-0 | 21-10 | +11 | 9p | [...] :   For detailed match results, click here."], "news_summary": "Cloud9 and Outlast are competing in the NAL 2026 Kickoff Group B, where both teams have started poorly. Cloud9 is 0-2 with a -9 round differential (5-14 rounds), while Outlast is also 0-2 with a -11 round differential (3-14 rounds). Both teams currently sit at the bottom of the standings with 0 points. This BO1 matchup represents a critical opportunity for either team to secure their first win of the season.", "side_yes_case": ["Cloud9 has slightly better recent performance with -9 round differential vs Outlast's -11", "Cloud9 showed competitive rounds against stronger opponents (DarkZero, Spacestation) despite losses", "Head-to-head context suggests Cloud9 may have edge in team infrastructure and experience", "Outlast has been significantly outscored (3-14 rounds), indicating more fundamental issues"], "side_no_case": ["Outlast and Cloud9 are evenly matched in overall record (both 0-2) and points (both 0p)", "Cloud9's 5-14 round count indicates severe competitive struggles matching Outlast's difficulty", "Both teams are performing poorly in the same tournament environment against the same opponents", "Outlast may have motivation advantage as clear underdog needing first win"], "key_context": ["Both teams are in Group B of NAL 2026 Kickoff Stage 1 regular season", "This is a Best-of-One (BO1) format match, increasing variance and upset potential", "Both teams are winless and pointless; this is a direct competition for first win", "DarkZero (3-0) and Spacestation Gaming (2-1) are leading the group, showing competitive level", "Recent playday results show matches were played in early April 2026"], "data_freshness": "2026-04-09T22:16:55.278974Z"}}, "resolution": {"resolved_at": "2026-04-10T06:15:00Z", "winning_side": "YES", "final_yes_price": 1.0, "checked_at": "2026-04-10T09:42:08.181879+00:00", "source": "gamma_api"}, "analysis": null}
{"run_id": "ed7b1da0-8b1f-4476-9775-8c79feb5c75f", "timestamp": "2026-04-09T22:18:00.677449+00:00", "legacy": false, "result": {"market": {"id": "1921945", "question": "Counter-Strike: shimmer vs insane players (BO3) - CCT North America Series #4 Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Elimination match between shimmer and insane players in the CCT North America Series #4 Group B, initially scheduled for April 9 at 8:30PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"shimmer\" if shimmer win the match against insane players.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"insane players\" if insane players win the match against shimmer.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-10T06:30:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T00:30:00Z", "yes_price": 0.12, "volume_usd": 35.252356, "category": "counter-strike"}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.12, "confidence_yes": 0.25, "confidence_no": 0.28, "rationale": "OUTCOME CONDITION: shimmer must win a BO3 match vs insane players in CCT NA Series #4 Group B, scheduled April 9 8:30PM ET (TODAY, ~8 hours until resolution). DATA QUALITY ASYMMETRY: The briefing contains minimal actionable intelligence\u2014no team statistics, recent form data, head-to-head records, player rosters, or performance metrics for either competitor. MARKET PRICING ANALYSIS: Current market prices show 88% confidence in insane players (NO), 12% confidence in shimmer (YES). This 7.3:1 odds ratio suggests strong market conviction, but the briefing offers NO evidence to justify such asymmetry. CASE FOR SHIMMER (YES): Briefing notes 'organized team structure' but provides no supporting data. No wins, losses, map records, or player caliber cited. Absence of negative information is not positive evidence. CASE FOR INSANE PLAYERS (NO): Equally underdocumented. No 'competitive history documented in search results' per briefing, yet market prices insane players as heavy favorite. This disconnect suggests market inefficiency or out-of-briefing knowledge. CRITICAL DATA GAPS: Briefing explicitly states 'no performance statistics or recent form data available' and 'no head-to-head matchup data.' This is disqualifying for confident probability estimation on either side. EVENT TIMING: Market resolves in ~8 hours (TODAY). Insufficient time for team announcements or major roster changes that could flip outcome probabilities. Live match outcome is imminent and largely determined by pre-match conditions. RESOLUTION MECHANISM: Criteria are clear (winner takes all, with forfeits/cancellations resolving 50-50). Team name 'shimmer' and 'insane players' appear sufficiently distinct and unambiguous. HLTV.org will provide definitive source. No mechanism risk identified. CONFIDENCE CALIBRATION: probability_score of 12 reflects market pricing (strong NO consensus), but confidence_yes=25 and confidence_no=28 both reflect data inadequacy. Neither side has substantive evidentiary support in the briefing. A genuine 50-50 bet (with low confidence on both sides) would be more honest, but market prices suggest insane players is favored; absent contradictory briefing data, defer slightly to market while flagging severe information asymmetry. FINAL ASSESSMENT: Assign probability_score=12 to match market pricing, but mark both confidence scores LOW (<30) to reflect the absence of competitive intelligence that would justify the market's 7.3:1 conviction spread.", "key_factor": "Critically insufficient competitive data on either team; market price (12% YES) reflects extreme confidence in insane players but briefing provides no substantive evidence to support or refute either team's likelihood of winning."}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.15, "confidence_yes": 0.25, "confidence_no": 0.72, "rationale": "FAVORED SIDE (NO = insane players) STRENGTHS: Market has priced insane players at 88% \u2014 an 7.3:1 odds ratio \u2014 suggesting strong underlying confidence in their victory. This wide spread indicates the market consensus views insane players as significantly favored, likely based on unobserved team strength, recent form, or seeding within the CCT tournament structure. UNDERDOG WEAKNESSES (YES = shimmer): The briefing provides almost no substantive data about Shimmer's recent performance, win rate, player roster quality, or form heading into this match. The 'case for YES' relies only on the existence of 'organized team structure' \u2014 an extremely weak foundation for a 12% allocation in a competitive BO3. DATA ASYMMETRY HEAVILY FAVORS NO: The briefing explicitly states 'No performance statistics or recent form data available for either team' and 'Lack of head-to-head matchup data.' This absence of counter-evidence against insane players means the market's consensus (88%) likely reflects ground-truth information not captured in the search results. The market is pricing based on real competitive data; the briefing's silence does not undermine insane players' advantage. MARKET PRICING AS SIGNAL: An 88% price in a direct head-to-head match with $35 volume is a strong consensus signal. Polymarket traders with access to HLTV, team rosters, recent VODs, and competitive ladder standings have converged on insane players. The absence of contradictory evidence in the briefing suggests no major upset catalyst (roster changes, visa issues, disqualifications) has emerged. SHIMMER LACKS POSITIVE CATALYSTS: The briefing contains no evidence of recent Shimmer momentum, roster upgrades, recent tournament wins, or tactical innovations. The sole positive note \u2014 'organized team structure' \u2014 is table-stakes for a CCT-level team and does not justify overturning a 7:1 market consensus. BO3 FORMAT REDUCES UPSET PROBABILITY: Best-of-3 inherently favors the stronger team more than BO1 would. If insane players has a skill edge (reflected in the 88% price), they have two chances to close out; Shimmer would need to steal two maps, a higher bar. TIMING & RESOLUTION RISK: Match resolves TODAY in ~8 hours. No time for new information, roster changes, or unforeseen circumstances to swing odds toward the underdog. The market's 88% is its final position barring a live reversal during the match itself. CONFIDENCE CALIBRATION: confidence_yes = 25 (weak positive case for Shimmer; only argument is 'organized structure'); confidence_no = 72 (strong market signal, absence of counter-evidence, BO3 format favors favorite, no upset catalysts identified). The asymmetry reflects that the underdog's case relies entirely on the briefing's lack of negative data \u2014 not active evidence they can win.", "key_factor": "insane players is priced as the overwhelming market favorite at 88%, reflecting the market's assessment that insane players is the stronger team in this CCT North America Series #4 BO3 matchup."}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.18, "confidence_yes": 0.28, "confidence_no": 0.32, "rationale": "MARKET STRUCTURE VULNERABILITY: The 88-12 split reflects near-certainty, yet the briefing contains zero hard data on either team's recent form, map pools, head-to-head records, or player rosters. This is a red flag for mispricing rather than confident favorite status. INFORMATION ASYMMETRY: NO side (insane players) is priced as a heavy favorite with no supporting evidence in the briefing. The search results explicitly state 'no detailed statistics, recent form data, or head-to-head records are provided.' A 76-point spread requires substantial performance differentiation \u2014 none is documented. BRIEFING QUALITY FAILURE: The 'KEY FACTS' section is nearly incoherent (Australian timezone complaints, random Finnish 1.6 references, unrelated tier-1 discussions). This suggests the briefing source itself lacks granular competitive intelligence on these specific teams. Markets often overprice favorites when data is sparse and default to historical narrative rather than actual matchup strength. SHIMMER CASE IGNORED: The briefing notes 'Shimmer team overview available suggests organized team structure' but then provides zero details. An organized CCT team with proper infrastructure is not inherently weak \u2014 this positive signal is present but completely discounted by the market. TIER-2 COMPETITIVENESS: Both teams compete in CCT NA Series 4 with 12-team structure and $10k prize pool. At this tier, variance is HIGH. BO3 format can reward a prepared, upset-ready roster. 12% implies shimmer is nearly twice as weak as insane players \u2014 unjustified by any comparative data. TIMING PRESSURE: Match resolves in ~8 hours. This is live/imminent, meaning sharp bettors likely placed action already based on private intel (Discord, coach networks, scrim results) not in the public briefing. However, the briefing's complete lack of competitive context suggests even sharp money may be absent \u2014 shallow market behavior. FAVORITE'S FRAGILITY: insane players' 88% price rests on zero documented recent wins, map statistics, or player form. This is not confidence; it is default assumption. In tier-2 CS, default assumptions collapse when a prepared underdog arrives with anti-strats or hot form. HIDDEN STRENGTH SCENARIO: Shimmer may have won recent scrims, fixed roster issues, or studied insane players' limited map pool. The briefing contains no information ruling this out. Conversely, insane players may be fielding a depleted lineup or in internal conflict \u2014 again, nothing in briefing confirms stability. BO3 UPSET MATH: In a BO3, an underdog at 40% true skill per map compounds to ~16% chance to win series. A 12% market price assumes shimmer is weaker than a 35% per-map baseline. That is steep for teams with unknown relative strength. MARKET DEPTH: $35 USD volume is shallow. This may reflect genuine edge (sharp money on insane players), OR it may reflect a knowledge vacuum where casual bettors just pick the 'stronger-sounding' name. No way to distinguish from briefing alone. CONSERVATIVE CONFIDENCE: Confidence_yes set to 28 (low) because the briefing provides almost NO positive data on shimmer \u2014 only absence of negative data. Confidence_no set to 32 (equally low) because insane players' favorite status rests entirely on narrative void, not documented form. Both sides are under-evidenced. PROBABILITY RECOMMENDATION: 18% for YES reflects a modest upside case for shimmer (organizing team, BO3 variance, information void favoring upset) while respecting that an 88% market price sometimes contains private intel. But the lack of public support for the favorite makes true probability likely HIGHER than 12% and closer to 25-30% range. Set at 18 as conservative bear estimate acknowledging data poverty.", "key_factor": "Extreme market mispricing driven by information vacuum \u2014 12% odds on a BO3 match between two tier-2 NA teams with zero documented performance differentials in the briefing."}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.12, "confidence_yes": 0.22, "confidence_no": 0.24, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: Briefing contains no meaningful performance metrics for either team \u2014 no recent match results, map win rates, player ratings, or head-to-head history. Market pricing (12% YES / 88% NO) heavily favors insane players, but this skew cannot be validated against actual team strength without comparative data. Both teams appear to be mid-tier or lower-tier NA Counter-Strike competitors in a CCT tournament (prize pool $10k, 12 teams) \u2014 not Tier-1 established organizations. Briefing includes fragmented community commentary with no actionable intelligence: references to map odds (1.4\u20131.7), vague form mentions ('quick 10-4 10-13'), and unsubstantiated claims about team tier status. Search results explicitly note 'limited specific information' and absence of 'detailed statistics, recent form data, or head-to-head records.' EVENT TIMING (TODAY, ~8 hours to resolution): Match is imminent; no major reversals in team composition or condition are likely to occur. Market may reflect informed trader knowledge (community betting syndicates, team insiders, or LAN qualifiers), but this cannot be verified from briefing alone. Confidence scores kept LOW on both sides (22, 24) because insufficient data exists to defensibly estimate either P(YES) or P(NO). The 12% price may be accurate, but it is not derived from documented evidence in the briefing. Probability score aligned conservatively with market price (12%) as a default position, but this reflects epistemic humility rather than conviction.", "key_factor": "Without reliable head-to-head data, recent form statistics, or team ranking differentials for lower-tier NA Counter-Strike teams, historical base rates cannot be reliably established. CCT tournaments feature semi-professional/amateur teams with highly variable performance."}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.12, "confidence_yes": 0.25, "confidence_no": 0.28, "rationale": "Extreme price asymmetry (12% YES / 88% NO) with minimal volume ($35 USD) suggests market confidence is driven by absence of data rather than positive conviction about insane players. Briefing contains zero performance metrics, recent form data, head-to-head records, or player roster information for either team \u2014 both sides operate in information vacuum. Current 12% YES price reflects a severe discount that would require insane players to be tier-1 dominant, but briefing provides no evidence of competitive tier-ranking for either squad. News summary explicitly states 'limited specific information' and 'no performance statistics or recent form data' \u2014 market may be pricing shimmer as underdog purely due to unfamiliarity rather than objective skill differential. Low volume and TODAY event timing (8 hours to resolution) means price reflects minimal liquidity and potentially shallow betting interest, not genuine crowd sentiment. No contradictory signals in briefing: neither team has documented recent losses, upsets, roster changes, or injury reports that would justify extreme confidence in insane players. CCT Series 4 Group B structure with 12 teams and $10K prize pool suggests competitive parity typical of mid-tier North American tournaments \u2014 not a tier-1 vs tier-2 mismatch. Confidence on both sides suppressed due to complete absence of differentiating data: cannot confidently assess why market favors insane players nor why shimmer might upset.", "key_factor": "Apathy"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.15, "confidence_yes": 0.25, "confidence_no": 0.28, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: No recent match statistics, win rates, map pool performance, or roster stability data for either shimmer or insane players\u2014standard HLTV/Liquipedia records are absent from briefing. MARKET PRICE AS SIGNAL: The 12% YES / 88% NO split is extreme but not grounded in documented team performance; this appears to reflect speculation or community perception rather than statistical analysis of comparable tier-2 CCT teams. TOURNAMENT CONTEXT: CCT North America Series 4 is a $10k regional qualifier\u2014participants are typically semi-pro or aspirational tier-2 teams with inconsistent rosters and high match volatility; outcome predictability is inherently low for such competitions. BO3 FORMAT RISK: Best-of-3 in low-tier competitive CS is highly sensitive to map vetoes, individual player form on the day, and tactical preparation; without roster/recent form data, directional confidence cannot justify >40% on either side. TEAM IDENTIFICATION AMBIGUITY: 'shimmer' and 'insane players' are generic names with no distinctive roster or sponsorship data provided; no verification that these are stable, documented teams on HLTV or Liquipedia. TIME-TO-EVENT DECAY: Market resolves in ~8 hours; the extremely low YES price (12%) suggests market has made a judgment, but given absence of supporting evidence, this may reflect narrative/bias rather than predictive accuracy. CONFIDENCE PENALTY: Both confidence_yes and confidence_no are set \u226430 because briefing provides zero comparative strength metrics, recent head-to-head records, or map-specific data\u2014the assignment of 15% to YES reflects the market signal, but conviction is low.", "key_factor": "The market data is severely insufficient\u2014neither team has documented competitive history, recent form metrics, or head-to-head records available; the 88% NO price reflects market skepticism toward shimmer rather than confidence in insane players, signaling both teams are likely tier-2/3 regional competitors with unknown relative strength."}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.14, "confidence_yes": 0.28, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: The briefing contains virtually no actionable competitive intelligence on either team\u2014no recent form, no head-to-head records, no player stats, no map pool analysis. This makes high confidence impossible on either side. MARKET SIGNAL INTERPRETATION: The 12% YES price suggests very strong market consensus that insane players are favorites. However, such extreme pricing (88% NO) on an obscure regional qualifier is often driven by liquidity constraints and small sample size rather than robust analysis. ASYMMETRIC CONFIDENCE JUSTIFICATION: confidence_no (35%) is deliberately held LOW because the briefing provides zero affirmative evidence about insane players' strength\u2014only the absence of information about shimmer. This is not confidence; it is default assumption. ROSTER/LINEUP BLIND SPOT: The briefing is completely silent on recent roster changes, player absences due to illness/injury, or stand-in substitutions for either team. In regional qualifiers with low media coverage, a key player absence can invert match outcomes. MAP POOL MISMATCH: No information on which maps each team prefers, bans, or has recently scrimmed. A shimmer team that has been grinding a specific map pool favored by their composition could exploit insane players' preparation gaps. MOTIVATION ASYMMETRY: Unknown whether insane players are already through to the next stage (lower seed, less pressure to win) or if shimmer is fighting for survival. Elimination psychology can flip close series. TIER-2 QUALIFIER VOLATILITY: CCT regional qualifiers feature teams with inconsistent scrim activity and public match data. The 12% price assumes competence parity; shimmer may have a structural edge (coaching, analyst, recent bootcamp) invisible in public records. FORM TRAJECTORY UNKNOWN: No recent wins/losses, no streak data. Shimmer could be in upward trajectory (won last 3 scrims) while market defaults to the name it recognizes (insane players). PROBABILITY RATIONALE: Set at 14 (just above the market's 12%) to reflect genuine uncertainty and the possibility of an unmeasured shimmer strength. The 2-point differential reflects contrarian skepticism of extreme pricing on data-poor matchups, not confidence in shimmer's objective strength. WHY NOT HIGHER: 14 is still well below 50 because 88% NO market consensus, while information-poor, is directionally consistent with the few signals available (no positive case for shimmer in briefing). A true black swan would require active evidence of shimmer advantage; absence of evidence is not evidence of absence, but it is not evidence of presence either.", "key_factor": "Shimmer's stand-in player or recent roster adjustment gives them unexpected tactical flexibility or map-pool advantage that contradicts the 12% market pricing."}], "final_score": 0.14101123595505619, "no_score": 0.857246963562753, "edge": 0.02101123595505619, "no_edge": -0.02275303643724702, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "LOW", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "NO-side confidence too low: 35% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1921945", "question": "Counter-Strike: shimmer vs insane players (BO3) - CCT North America Series #4 Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Elimination match between shimmer and insane players in the CCT North America Series #4 Group B, initially scheduled for April 9 at 8:30PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"shimmer\" if shimmer win the match against insane players.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"insane players\" if insane players win the match against shimmer.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-10T06:30:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T00:30:00Z", "yes_price": 0.12, "volume_usd": 35.252356, "category": "counter-strike"}, "key_facts": ["Maybe because not everyone lives in the Americas and Europe?", "Us aussies and asians never get to watch a grandfinal because they always take place during", "It was, but they got the name from an old Finnish 1.6 team.", "The current eyeballers actually have nothing to do with the old one either, JW and Flusha bought", "in