{"run_id": "7c221666-1da2-44e9-85be-692edbbedc3d", "timestamp": "2026-04-10T12:14:45.190371+00:00", "legacy": false, "result": {"market": {"id": "1933630", "question": "LoL: HMBLE vs StoneHenge Esports (BO1) - LIT Regular Season", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the LoL match between HMBLE and StoneHenge Esports in the LIT Regular Season, initially scheduled for April 10 at 2:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"HMBLE\" if HMBLE win the match against StoneHenge Esports.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"StoneHenge Esports\" if StoneHenge Esports win the match against HMBLE.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://gol.gg/esports/home. However, if https://gol.gg/esports/home has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-11T00:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T18:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.88, "volume_usd": 1055.573884, "category": "league-of-legends"}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.72, "confidence_yes": 0.68, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "RESOLUTION CONDITION: Match must be played to completion with HMBLE winning; forfeits/walkovers resolve 50-50, cancellations/delays >7 days resolve 50-50. HMBLE PERFORMANCE DATA: Top-tier metrics demonstrate competitive strength - 2280 GPM (tied 4th in split), players with 18-kill individual performances, victories over P11 Esports and competitive engagement with EKO Esports/Colossal Gaming. STONEHENGE ESPORTS DATA GAP: Zero performance statistics in briefing prevents direct comparison. No GPM, KDA, win records, or player skill metrics available. This is the primary data asymmetry driving analysis uncertainty. MARKET PRICING SIGNAL: 88% YES / 12% NO suggests market has significant information about StoneHenge (likely negative) or high confidence in HMBLE superiority. However, 88% may overweight HMBLE given BO1 variance and complete absence of opponent data. BO1 FORMAT RISK: Single-game format increases variance and eliminates adaptation. HMBLE's game duration variance (24:11 to 56:43) suggests execution inconsistency that could be exploited in single game. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: High confidence in YES assessment (68) reflects strong HMBLE metrics. Low confidence in NO assessment (35) reflects data void on StoneHenge - cannot properly evaluate their likelihood of winning without any performance information. PROBABILITY CALIBRATION: 72% reflects HMBLE's demonstrated top-tier performance while discounting for (1) missing opponent data, (2) BO1 format variance, (3) execution inconsistency shown in game duration spreads. Market's 88% appears overconfident given information gaps. RESOLUTION MECHANISM: Official source gol.gg with 2-hour fallback to credible reporting. Team name alignment acceptable (HMBLE and StoneHenge clearly identifiable). No ambiguity risk in resolution criteria.", "key_factor": "Complete absence of StoneHenge Esports performance data prevents balanced assessment of the NO side; market pricing at 88% YES suggests significant information asymmetry favoring HMBLE."}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.82, "confidence_yes": 0.78, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "HMBLE ranks 4th in GPM (2280) tied with Colossal Gaming, placing them in the upper echelon of the LIT 2026 Winter Split \u2014 a reliable proxy for macro execution and team coordination in BO1 contexts. HMBLE players show elite individual mechanics: Tyrone and Vigil posted 18-kill performances against EKO Esports (the split's top GPM team at 2423), demonstrating carry capacity in high-stakes matchups. HMBLE's recent wins include 2-0 victories over BCE and 3BL Esports in group stages, and competitive performance vs. Colossal Gaming (2280 GPM, tied with HMBLE), confirming viability against peers. StoneHenge Esports appears entirely absent from the provided statistical summaries, performance tables, and match history across the LIT 2026 Winter and Spring datasets \u2014 this is a critical red flag for an underdog in a high-confidence market. BO1 format typically favors teams with deeper preparation and established playbooks; HMBLE's consistent appearances in the data suggest higher organizational visibility and competitive frequency. The 88% market price reflects a strong consensus view; at this level, the market is pricing StoneHenge as a significant underperformer or newly-promoted/low-seeded team with minimal recorded competitive history. HMBLE's game duration variance (24:11 to 56:43) indicates flexible strategic execution rather than brittleness \u2014 ability to execute fast wins and grind out long games shows adaptability. However, lack of direct StoneHenge Esports performance metrics prevents confident assessment of their specific weaknesses, ceiling, or recent form \u2014 this data gap caps confidence_no at 45 due to unknown underdog factors. If StoneHenge Esports were a competitive threat, their stats would likely appear in the shared LIT 2026 dataset; their absence in top-performer tables and match histories suggests lower competitive tier or participation level. Market efficiency: the 88% YES price reflects both HMBLE's documented strength and the market's inability to construct a strong case for StoneHenge without available performance data.", "key_factor": "HMBLE's demonstrated top-tier economic metrics (2280 GPM, tied 4th) and proven ability to compete against elite teams combined with complete absence of StoneHenge Esports performance data in the competitive record."}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.18, "confidence_yes": 0.35, "confidence_no": 0.72, "rationale": "HMBLE STRENGTHS: Top-tier economic metrics (2280 GPM, tied 4th best in split); high-impact individual performances (Tyrone, Vigil with 18-kill games); demonstrated ability to compete against elite teams (EKO, Colossal Gaming); secured recent victories vs comparable opponents (P11 Esports 2-0). HMBLE VULNERABILITIES: Extreme game duration variance (24:11 to 56:43) indicates inconsistent execution and potential drafting/macro weaknesses; loses early-game momentum frequently (fastest win 24:11 vs P11 suggests vulnerability to organized early pressure); BO1 format punishes inconsistency\u2014no adaptation available. STONEHENGE CRITICAL DATA GAP: Zero performance statistics available in briefing\u2014no GPM data, no recent match results, no player KDAs, no draft patterns. This is the strongest upset indicator: market is pricing 88% YES despite inability to properly evaluate the NO side. MARKET MISPRICING SIGNAL: 88% YES probability on a BO1 match with one team having no available performance data suggests overconfidence in HMBLE or simple name recognition. Professional esports BO1s routinely produce upsets when matchup data is asymmetric. UPSET SCENARIO VIABILITY: If StoneHenge has developed strong early-game coordination or counter-prepared for HMBLE's economic playstyle, the variance in HMBLE's game durations (indicating macro inconsistency) creates a legitimate upset pathway. BO1 format amplifies this\u2014no recovery possible. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY JUSTIFIED: High confidence in HMBLE's demonstrated strengths (confidence_yes=35) but LOW confidence that 88% properly reflects the NO side's actual capabilities (confidence_no=72). The data gap on StoneHenge means we cannot confidently reject their upset chances despite the market pricing. TIME PRESSURE CONSIDERATION: Event is TODAY, resolving in ~12 hours. Very little time for information updates. If StoneHenge has prepared specifically for this matchup and HMBLE relies on inconsistent macro execution, execution in the single game matters enormously. No comebacks available. BO1 VOLATILITY FACTOR: Tournament average is 36 kills/game over 31:58 duration. HMBLE's 24-minute and 56-minute extremes suggest they are vulnerable to early snowballing OR extended teamfight attrition. A team exploiting either weakness in a single game can capitalize fully. VERDICT: 88% YES is overpriced given complete absence of StoneHenge data. HMBLE is the stronger known quantity, but their inconsistency combined with BO1 format and missing opponent information justifies meaningful upset probability. 18% for NO reflects market overconfidence, not analytical certainty.", "key_factor": "Complete absence of StoneHenge Esports performance data prevents confident assessment of the 88% YES pricing, suggesting market may be overweighting HMBLE's name recognition or historical standing."}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.72, "confidence_yes": 0.68, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "HMBLE ranks 4th in LIT 2026 Winter GPM (2280, tied with Colossal Gaming), indicating strong macro economic execution\u2014a historically reliable predictor of BO1 victory in regional LoL leagues. HMBLE players demonstrate elite individual mechanics: Tyrone and Vigil each recorded 18-kill performances; Bisgaard (20.8 KDA) and Piskhello (16.2 KDA) rank in top-5 KDA leaderboard, supporting consistent high-kill engagement. HMBLE competitive record shows wins against established opponents (P11 Esports in 24:11, engagement with EKO/CG); regular season performance against known opponents is stronger predictor than one-off BO1 variance. StoneHenge Esports: zero performance data available in briefing document\u2014no GPM, KDA, win-loss record, or head-to-head history provided. This data asymmetry prevents reliable NO-side probability estimation. BO1 format inherently increases variance (~8-12pp vs BO3/BO5), but does NOT erase skill differential; HMBLE's top-4 macro metrics and elite player KDA suggest structural advantage that persists in single-game format. Historical BO1 base rate: ~70-75% win probability for statistically superior team; HMBLE's economic/statistical edge (2280 GPM, 4th-ranked) supports confidence in YES-side. Game duration variance (HMBLE: 24:11 to 56:43) reflects drafting/opponent adaptation, not mechanical failure; no evidence of consistent execution breakdown. Briefing provides robust HMBLE historical data (28 games, multi-tournament context) but ZERO StoneHenge context, creating asymmetric confidence: HIGH on YES (HMBLE proven), LOW on NO (opponent unknown). Time-to-resolution: ~12 hours remaining; no major reversals (e.g., roster changes, COVID, technical failures) documented in briefing. High confidence reflects match timing proximity.", "key_factor": "In BO1 esports matches, the higher-seeded or statistically stronger team wins ~70-75% of the time; BO1 format increases variance but favors established favorites by ~8-12 percentage points over BO5/BO3"}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.72, "confidence_yes": 0.75, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "HMBLE objective strength: ranked 4th in GPM (2280, tied with Colossal Gaming), top-tier economic performance in split with 28 games of data. Players demonstrate high-end individual skill (Tyrone/Vigil 18-kill performances). This supports YES at 72-75%. StoneHenge Esports: zero performance data in briefing\u2014no GPM, KDA, win records, or recent match results available. Complete information asymmetry means NO side cannot be properly evaluated. Confidence_no suppressed to 35 due to unknown underdog risk. Market price 88% YES is moderately aggressive but not irrational. HMBLE's top-4 GPM ranking and demonstrated competitive wins justify 70-75% baseline. The 88% price suggests market believes HMBLE is heavily favored, which aligns with available data showing them as established competitor. BO1 format increases variance and reversal risk. HMBLE's game duration range (24:11 to 56:43) and mixed execution in extended games indicate they can struggle with pacing consistency. Single game eliminates adaptation advantage but also prevents comeback. This is priced into the 88% as a risk factor already. No head-to-head history or recent StoneHenge roster/form data in briefing. Without benchmarking StoneHenge against split-level teams, cannot justify probability below 70%. Possible StoneHenge is a strong team playing poorly in metrics, or underperforming known team. Uncertainty prevents confidence_no from rising above 35. Crowd sentiment appears Rational, not Greed-driven. Market is pricing HMBLE as clear favorite but not at lottery odds (would be 95%+). The 88% reflects legitimate performance gap based on available data while acknowledging single-game variance. Time-to-reversal: Event is TODAY (12 hours to resolution). At 88% YES, a StoneHenge upset would require exceptional execution or HMBLE collapse. BO1 format means no second chances; early draft/execution mistakes are fatal. 88% reflects low but non-negligible reversal probability in BO1 context. Asymmetric confidence justified: HMBLE data is rich (GPM, KDA, game logs, opponent quality). StoneHenge data is absent. Confidence_yes=75 reflects solid HMBLE fundamentals. Confidence_no=35 reflects data void, not weakness of NO case itself.", "key_factor": "Rational"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.72, "confidence_yes": 0.76, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "HMBLE economic dominance is real (2280 GPM, tied 4th split-wide) but GPM in isolation does not predict BO1 wins\u2014it measures efficiency mid-game, not conversion or macro discipline in best-of-one scenarios where a single draft/execution failure is terminal. Game-duration volatility (24:11 vs P11 to 56:43 vs EKO Esports) is a critical red flag in competitive LoL: extreme variance signals either inconsistent draft execution or inability to close advantages cleanly\u2014BO1 format punishes both severely. Individual player peaks (Tyrone 18 kills, Vigil 18 kills) demonstrate mechanical skill but in isolated high-kill games (88 kills total vs EKO); this style is high-variance and prone to coordination breakdowns in tightly-drafted matchups. Zero available data on StoneHenge Esports performance is asymmetric risk: they could be an upset-capable lower-tier squad (common in regional EU leagues like LIT) or a mid-table consistent performer\u2014inability to assess their stability or anti-HMBLE preparation reduces confidence in the YES side despite HMBLE's rating advantage. BO1 inherently favors teams with superior draft-to-execution alignment and reduces sample size for variance correction; HMBLE's inconsistency in game length suggests macro instability that translates to BO1 vulnerability. Market price (88% YES / 12% NO) reflects information asymmetry: HMBLE is a known quantity with documented stats; StoneHenge anonymity may suppress their perceived value artificially, but this does not confirm HMBLE win probability objectively. Tournament context: LIT is a B-Tier regional league; team quality disparity is steeper than top-tier competition, meaning upsets are more common and a 'favored' team's statistical edge is less predictive than in Tier-1 tournaments. Event timing (TODAY, ~12 hours to resolution) means no new information will materially shift outcome probability; current market price reflects pre-game sentiment rather than updated intel on team form, roster changes, or day-of preparation. Confidence asymmetry justified: HMBLE performance data is detailed and quantifiable (76% confidence_yes), but absence of StoneHenge intel creates structural uncertainty about their meta positioning, recent results, and coach/player synergy (45% confidence_no reflects genuine data gap, not balanced uncertainty).", "key_factor": "HMBLE's BO1 vulnerability stems from extreme game-duration variance (24:11 to 56:43) indicating draft-dependent execution and macro consistency issues that single-elimination format punishes harshly, whereas their strong GPM ranking reflects mid-game resource efficiency rather than win-condition closing reliability."}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.72, "confidence_yes": 0.68, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "HMBLE's massive game duration variance (24:11 to 56:43) signals execution inconsistency and potential draft/playstyle vulnerability\u2014BO1 format punishes teams that cannot lock in a single coherent gameplan. Complete absence of StoneHenge Esports performance data in briefing is a critical blind spot. They could be a rising challenger with fresh meta knowledge or a team HMBLE has not prepared adequately for. HMBLE's 2280 GPM ranking is respectable but not dominant\u2014tied for 4th, not top-3. Economic advantage alone does not guarantee BO1 victory, especially if opponent executes early-game advantage or macro control. Briefing shows HMBLE's pentakill leader (Divine, 1 pentakill) and multi-kill consistency, but also reveals they play extended, resource-heavy games. A BO1 punishes teams reliant on scaling and team fight cleanup. No head-to-head or recent matchup history provided. StoneHenge may have scrimmed HMBLE extensively or identified a specific counter-comp that the briefing data does not surface. Market pricing at 88% YES is extremely confident but based on incomplete information (no opponent statistics). This overconfidence suggests YES side is pricing in false certainty. HMBLE's wins shown are against P11 (24:11, routable early) and competitive BO5 context (playoffs vs CG). Regular season BO1 vs unknown opponent introduces unquantified risk. Absence of StoneHenge stats could indicate they are a newer/lower-seeded team with different preparation cycle, meta read, or roster cohesion that catches HMBLE off-guard in a single-elimination game.", "key_factor": "StoneHenge Esports executes a patch-tuned composition or meta-shift strategy that exploits HMBLE's historically inconsistent drafting and execution in high-variance BO1 formats."}], "final_score": 0.6962820512820513, "no_score": 0.39384105960264904, "edge": -0.18371794871794866, "no_edge": 0.27384105960264904, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "YES-side confidence too low: 67% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1933630", "question": "LoL: HMBLE vs StoneHenge Esports (BO1) - LIT Regular Season", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the LoL match between HMBLE and StoneHenge Esports in the LIT Regular Season, initially scheduled for April 10 at 2:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"HMBLE\" if HMBLE win the match against StoneHenge Esports.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"StoneHenge Esports\" if StoneHenge Esports win the match against HMBLE.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://gol.gg/esports/home. However, if https://gol.gg/esports/home has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-11T00:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T18:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.88, "volume_usd": 1055.573884, "category": "league-of-legends"}, "key_facts": ["| Top Multikill |\n\n| |  | Pentakill | Quadra | Triple | Double |  ---  ---  | Divine | 1 | 0 | 1 | 7 | | Bisgaard | 0 | 0 | 1 | 6 | | Vigil | 0 | 0 | 1 | 5 | | Piskhello | 0 | 0 | 1 | 4 | | Mario | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | |\n\n|  | Pentakill | Quadra | Triple | Double |\n ---  --- \n| Divine | 1 | 0 | 1 | 7 |\n| Bisgaard | 0 | 0 | 1 | 6 |\n| Vigil | 0 | 0 | 1 | 5 |\n