ng to justify the extreme 96% NO pricing; reversal to mean probability is highly likely once match concludes or becomes clearly favorable to one side.", "key_factor": "In competitive League of Legends, teams with 64% win rate vs 50% win rate opponent historically win single games ~70-75% of the time; head-to-head dominance (3+ consecutive wins) adds ~5-8pp; Game 1 unpredictability reduces edge by ~3-5pp."}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.72, "confidence_yes": 0.78, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "CROWD PANIC SIGNAL: YES side crashed from 59% EMA (7-day avg) to 4% current price \u2014 a 55-point collapse in 24 hours. Sharp -67pp move at Apr 13 09:59 UTC suggests panic selling, not rational repricing. FUNDAMENTAL DISCONNECT: Nongshim is objectively stronger \u2014 64% win rate vs Hanwha's 50%, ranked 3rd vs 5th, perfect head-to-head record (won 3-0, 3-2, and multiple regular season games). Yet market prices them at 4% to win a single game. OVERSOLD ON TIMING: YES fell only 6 hours before event. Massive volatility (27.5pp/hr) suggests reactive positioning rather than new information. Brief window to reversal remains but panic has clearly priced in worst-case scenario. GAME 1 CONTEXT UNDERVALUED: Market treats this as decisive outcome, but Game 1 in best-of series is often exploratory. Teams frequently adapt after first loss. Nongshim's playoff dominance (beat T1 1-3, KT 3-0, beat Hanwha 3-2 in playoffs) shows they execute when it matters. INDIVIDUAL PLAYER SUPERIORITY IGNORED: MihawK (69.6% jungle), SeTab (63.4% mid) vastly outpace typical challengers. These metrics should command >70% probability, yet market is at 4% \u2014 severe mispricing toward fearful crowd. HANWHA WEAKNESS CLEAR: 50% win rate, 5th place, 34:03 avg game duration (slowest = vulnerable macro), lower playoff seeding. No catalyst shown in briefing for sudden upset vs dominant opponent. COUNTERPOINT (modest): Game 1 unpredictability exists; best-of series do see upsets on G1. Hanwha has shown they can compete (won some matches). But 96% NO price implies Nongshim has <5% chance, which contradicts all performance data. RATIONAL REPRICING NEEDED: Fair value likely 65-75% YES (Nongshim). Current 4% reflects pure panic, not analysis. Early market price of 59% was closer to reality.", "key_factor": "Fear"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.72, "confidence_yes": 0.78, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "Nongshim's 64% win rate (7-4) vs. Hanwha's 50% (4-4) reflects substantial skill gap; 3rd vs. 5th seed ranking reinforces tier separation in LCK CL structure. Head-to-head record is overwhelmingly in Nongshim's favor across 2026 season: won 3-2 playoff series, 3-0 regular season sweep, and dominated individual Game 1 matchups (e.g., 61.5 vs. 48 team gold differential in playoffs R1G1). MihawK's jungle statistics (69.6% WR on Jarvan/Vi/Xin; 100% WR on Lee Sin/Sejuani/Trundle with exceptional KDAs) and SeTab's mid (63.4% WR, 80% on Ryze/Akali, 100% on Aurora/Galio/Syndra) indicate lane dominance in early skirmish patterns. Hanwha's longer average game duration (34:03 vs. Nongshim's 31:42) suggests macro vulnerability and reliance on late-game scaling rather than tempo control\u2014Nongshim typically closes out via superior mid-game execution. Sharp market move (-67pp YES in last hour before deadline) suggests informed traders repositioning on late-breaking information; however, with event resolving TODAY (~5 hours), this volatility reflects uncertainty rather than fundamental information, likely due to unknown draft/roster adjustments. Game 1 context: In best-of series, teams often play exploratory or psychologically loose Game 1s to test meta and opponent meta reads before high-stakes later games. However, Nongshim's recent playoff trajectory (3-0 vs. KT, 3-2 vs. HLE in R1) suggests confidence and momentum. Hanwha's 50% WR is inflated by weaker opposition; against top 3 teams (T1, Nongshim, KT), Hanwha shows significantly lower performance. Sample size of ~8-10 games against Nongshim heavily favors NS across all contexts (patch 16.1, 16.2, 16.3). Event timing (TODAY, resolves in ~5 hours) limits reversal likelihood: Nongshim would need to field a fundamentally weakened or roster-disrupted lineup to lose Game 1 to a 5th-seed opponent with no recent upset indicators. Confidence_yes is high (78) due to clear statistical dominance, head-to-head record, and individual player metrics; confidence_no is lower (45) because Hanwha's path to victory is primarily via unpredictable Game 1 strategy/draft fluke rather than baseline competitiveness\u2014insufficient data on HLE's recent adaptations or meta shifts favor them.", "key_factor": "Nongshim's dominance in jungle (MihawK 69.6% WR) and mid (SeTab 63.4% WR) control early-to-mid game tempo in a meta where these roles dictate skirmish patterns; however, Game 1 outcomes in best-of series are strategically unpredictable as teams frequently sacrifice early games to gather draft/meta intelligence before committing full preparation."}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.08, "confidence_yes": 0.22, "confidence_no": 0.78, "rationale": "Market price collapse to 4% YES in last 6 hours (-67pp at Apr 13 09:59 UTC) suggests either material negative information about Nongshim (injury, roster change, server/admin issue) OR sharper money recognizing the matchup imbalance. The briefing contains NO explanation for this magnitude swing, creating a data blind spot. Nongshim's dominance in head-to-head is extreme (3-2, 3-0, multiple wins) and nearly unambiguous. This level of consensus typically invites contrarian positioning and potential sharp action on the underdog. Game 1 of a series is structurally vulnerable to experimentation and intel-gathering. Hanwha may use Game 1 as a low-stakes probe to test Nongshim's adaptability on the current patch, then pivot for Games 2+. Playoff Game 1s often see unconventional picks. Briefing shows rich champion pool data for Nongshim (SeTab with 80% Ryze, 80% Akali) but provides NO comparable champion pool breakdown for Hanwha's mid-laner or detailed composition analysis. This asymmetry in visibility means Hanwha's pocket picks or meta-shift capability is entirely opaque. Jungle champion data (MihawK vs Hanwha junglers) is incomplete in briefing. If Hanwha's jungler has recently mastered a Lee Sin or Nocturne pick that crushes Jarvan IV (MihawK's 66.7% most-played), that meta matchup is invisible to this analysis. Patch 16.3 was in effect for most recent matches. If the match is on patch 16.4 or later (not stated), recent historical win rates become partially stale. Meta patches often flip underdog viability in 24-48 hours. Hanwha's 34:03 avg game duration vs. Nongshim's 31:42 is flagged as 'slower macro,' but could also indicate Hanwha plays for mid-to-late pivots where scaling and team cohesion trump early aggression. A single Game 1 teamfight or macro blunder from Nongshim in the 25-35min window could flip momentum. No mention of coaching changes, recent roster swaps, or player health/availability. Absence of injury reports or roster notes in a briefing with ~5 hours to match time is suspicious and raises risk of unmeasured personnel shifts. Market volume of $59.6k is moderate, not particularly liquid. Sharp actors may have exited YES positions ahead of match time once conviction on Hanwha weakness solidified, explaining the 24-hour collapse. Nongshim's recent T1 academy matchup record shows a 1-3 loss sandwiched between 3-0 and 2-1 wins, indicating variance and vulnerability to tactical counters even against academy teams. Hanwha is not guaranteed to perform worse than mid-tier academy rosters. If match is delayed, canceled, or Game 1 does not complete, resolution falls to 50-50 (per criteria). Time-to-match (~5 hours) is tight; any pre-match technical issue or player health concern becomes a small but real tail risk that inflates Hanwha's fair value.", "key_factor": "Hanwha's mid-laner or jungle player executes a patch-specific meta pivot (e.g., non-standard champion pool or off-meta pick) that Nongshim's rigid recent game plans have no precedent for countering, compounded by Game 1 being a tactical probe where Hanwha deliberately experiments to gather series intelligence."}], "final_score": 0.540327868852459, "no_score": 0.6278534031413613, "edge": 0.500327868852459, "no_edge": -0.3321465968586387, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "YES price too low: $0.04 (need \u2265$0.09)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1935073", "question": "LoL: Nongshim Esports Academy vs Hanwha Life Esports Challengers - Game 1 Winner", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the LoL match between Nongshim Esports Academy and Hanwha Life Esports Challengers in the LCK Challengers League Rounds 1-2, initially scheduled for April 13 at 5:00AM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Nongshim Esports Academy\" if Nongshim Esports Academy win Game 1 against Hanwha Life Esports Challengers.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Hanwha Life Esports Challengers\" if Hanwha Life Esports Challengers win Game 1 against Nongshim Esports Academy.\n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and Game 1 is concluded with a winner determined, this market will resolve based on the completed Game 1. \n\nIf Game 1 is not completed for any reason, this market will resolve 50-50. \n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all) or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without play beginning, this market will resolve 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://gol.gg/esports/home. However, if https://gol.gg/esports/home has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.", "end_date": "2026-04-13T15:10:00Z", "event_start_time"