no arbitrage signal. TOURNAMENT CONTEXT: IEM Rio is a premier LAN with 16 teams. Both teams are at the same venue/travel disadvantage, so regional proximity is neutral. Na'Vi's LAN experience advantage remains. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: High confidence in YES (72) because ranking data, stats, and historical precedent are clear and unambiguous. Lower confidence in NO (45) because B8's specific roster strength, recent momentum, and map preferences are not detailed in briefing\u2014underdog outcomes are inherently harder to predict when you lack granular form data. LIVE EVENT RISK: Match resolves in ~14 hours. Market has already incorporated public information. No significant time for reversal unless injury/forfeit (low probability per resolution rules). VERDICT: Probability aligned with base rate. Market reflects rational expectation of a tier-1 dominant performance in BO3 format.", "key_factor": "In professional Counter-Strike BO3 matches, tier-1 teams (top 5-10 ranking) defeat tier-2/3 teams (ranked 15+) approximately 80-85% of the time. Upsets occur in 15-20% of cases, typically when lower-ranked teams have recent form momentum or specific map advantages."}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.16, "confidence_yes": 0.85, "confidence_no": 0.78, "rationale": "Market price of 18% YES is appropriately reflective of objective skill disparity: Na'Vi ranked #2 globally vs B8 ranked #18 \u2014 a 16-place gap in tier-1 Counter-Strike. Statistical fundamentals align with market odds: Na'Vi's 1.16 rating + 74.5% KAST vs B8's 1.11 rating + 71.4% KAST shows measurable performance gap across key metrics. BO3 format amplifies favorite advantage \u2014 requires consistency across multiple maps, reducing upset probability vs single-map play; Na'Vi's proven LAN experience compounds this. No crowd emotion detected: briefing contains noise (unrelated FUT/Astralis commentary, Fisher College logistics) but no recent form disruptions, roster changes, or emotional catalysts favoring B8. Price stability over 7 days (-1.0pp change, 0.7pp/hr volatility) indicates market has settled on fundamentals rather than reacting to sentiment swings. Regional rivalry context (both Ukrainian teams) does NOT meaningfully reduce Na'Vi's advantage \u2014 skill tier still dominates in structured LAN competition. Minor asymmetry in confidence: HIGH confidence in YES (85) because objective ranking + stats + format clearly favor Na'Vi; confidence_no at 78 (not 95) because esports upsets exist (B8 could win 1-2 maps or exploit a specific anti-strat), but 16% base probability already reflects this tail risk. No ignored signals: briefing provides no late-roster changes, injury reports, or form reversals that would justify repricing against the market. Conclusion: Market is NOT mispriced. 18% for B8 reflects true underdog status with minimal realistic path to victory in BO3 vs a top-2 team.", "key_factor": "Rational"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.16, "confidence_yes": 0.85, "confidence_no": 0.42, "rationale": "RANKING DELTA MEANING: In professional Counter-Strike, a #2 vs #18 ranking gap is extreme\u2014not marginal. This reflects months of LAN results, head-to-head records, and map pool mastery. Na'Vi's #2 status is earned through consistent wins against tier-1 competition; B8's #18 reflects losses to similar tier-1 teams. STATISTICAL PREDICTABILITY: B8's 1.11 rating vs Na'Vi's 1.16 rating appears close numerically but conceals context\u2014Na'Vi achieves this against tier-1 opposition; B8 against weaker teams. Rating inflation at lower tiers is a known phenomenon. The 74.5% vs 71.4% KAST gap similarly understates Na'Vi's true superiority. BO3 FORMAT AMPLIFIES SKILL GAPS: Best-of-3 format requires consistency across 2-3 maps. Lower-ranked teams' upset chances are higher in single-map formats (BO1); BO3 forces teams to execute map pools and adapt mid-series. Na'Vi's coaching, anti-strat preparation, and mid-round calling depth will dominate across multiple maps. ROSTER PEDIGREE & IGL ADVANTAGE: Aleksib (Na'Vi's IGL) is a veteran in-game leader; B8's leadership structure is not detailed but implied weaker. Call-making under LAN pressure\u2014economy management, retake setups, anti-eco execution\u2014differentiates tier-1 from tier-2 teams decisively in BO3. TRAVEL/PREPARATION CONTEXT: Both teams are Eastern European competing in Brazil, but Na'Vi's resources (org funding, coaching staff, analyst support) far exceed B8's. This mitigates travel disadvantage for Na'Vi; B8 lacks institutional support to overcome fatigue or tactical surprise. HISTORICAL TIER-1 vs TIER-2 UPSET RATES: Major esports betting data shows tier-1 vs tier-2 upsets in BO3 format occur 10-15% of the time, not 18%. The market price reflects casual/sentiment betting rather than sharp analysis. 18% is inflated for a gap this wide. PLAYER CEILING GAP: Na'Vi's roster (including players calibrated to tier-1 peaks) have mechanical skill floors that B8 cannot match consistently. One bad map for Na'Vi is still competitive; one bad map for B8 often means a stomp, making BO3 recovery mathematically harder. TOURNAMENT STRUCTURE: Group B stage in a $1M premier tournament attracts strong teams. B8 likely qualified through regional qualifiers or lower brackets; this is a step up in competition. First major LAN exposure against #2 team in BO3 format is historically unfavorable for underdogs. MARKET PRICING INEFFICIENCY: 18% YES reflects fear of upsets (valid in esports) but overweights B8's narrative potential. Sharp betting typically prices #2 vs #18 BO3 at 8-12% for the underdog. The 18% suggests retail positioning favoring the upset story.", "key_factor": "In tier-1 CS:GO/CS2 BO3 matches, the #2 globally ranked team versus #18 typically wins 85-90% of the time; the 18% market price significantly overvalues B8's upset probability given the structural skill gap, map control knowledge disparity, and experience differential in high-pressure LAN environments."}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.16, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "Market is LIVE/IMMINENT (resolves in ~14 hours) \u2014 reversal window is extremely narrow; only personnel/logistical shocks can flip the outcome. Briefing contains ZERO mention of Na'Vi or B8 team health, visa status, or recent travel disruptions \u2014 conspicuous silence on pre-tournament logistics for a Brazil LAN where Eastern European teams face cross-timezone fatigue. Brazil location + April timing = potential visa delays, illness spread in confined tournament environment, or last-minute roster changes not yet public (common in esports 24h before matches). Briefing references Fisher College visa/scheduling conflict (April 8 note) as precedent \u2014 suggests travel complications ARE occurring at this tournament window. B8 ranked #18 vs Na'Vi #2 creates 82% market consensus, but BO3 format allows upsets if Na'Vi plays with substitute/undermanned roster; one roster hole cascades across three maps. Regional rivalry (both Ukrainian teams) + unfamiliar LAN environment could psychologically favor B8 if Na'Vi is distracted or fatigued; no morale/form trajectory data provided for either team. Briefing stats are 3-month aggregates \u2014 does NOT capture current form (pre-tournament week), individual player momentum, or recent map pool shifts favoring B8's style. News summary is generic tier-1 vs tier-2 framing; lacks specific recent head-to-head results, map veto history, or tactical innovations that could enable B8 upset. Market stability (YES at 19% \u00b11pp over 7 days) suggests NO consensus lock-in; significant asymmetric information gap exists pre-match, not yet priced. B8's 1.11 rating (within 2.2% of Na'Vi's 1.16) is closer than ranking gap suggests \u2014 underdog is NOT a complete skill mismatch; BO3 volatility could favor upset if map selection aligns.", "key_factor": "Na'Vi roster disruption or substitution due to visa/travel complications arriving in Brazil, or key player illness/injury (Aleksib or b1t) forcing emergency lineup adjustment hours before match."}], "final_score": 0.1690497737556561, "no_score": 0.8271759259259258, "edge": -0.01595022624434389, "no_edge": 0.012175925925925868, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "NO-side confidence too low: 62% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1842267", "question": "Counter-Strike: B8 vs Natus Vincere (BO3) - IEM Rio Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Upper bracket quarterfinal 2 match between B8 and Natus Vincere in the IEM Rio Group B, initially scheduled for April 13 at 10:00AM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"B8\" if B8 win the match against Natus Vincere.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Natus Vincere\" if Natus Vincere win the match against B8.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-14T00:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-13T18:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.185, "volume_usd": 71972.28440200009, "category": "counter-strike", "clob_token_ids": ["19468732107850826124910683854873553648656766893335941507216915527138203216208", "101687252252173407855874001007234632961004698674586298741585377230015905139695"]}, "key_facts": ["Welcome to the leading Counter-Strike site in the world, featuring news, demos, pictures, statistics, on-site coverage and much much more!", "FUT have laid claim to their first notable LAN trophy since entering the Counter-Strike scene nearly one year ago, taking down Astralis in the best-of-five", "Eyeballers, JW Gives Birth On Stage And The Child Is Carried By Donk And Zywoo.", "No Other Then Jame Will Be The Messiah And His Children To Suffer The Sin Of", "If team dont win nothing, theyre not tier1?", "Bro, so CCT tournaments who won it, tier1 teams?", "Bro ur brain so small, now era vitality, they winning", "hope fut wins, this team has so much potential, winning a trophy might keep them together, hopefully no other org buys some of their players,", "karrigan's stint with FaZe is coming to a close after the team's disastrous IEM Cologne qualifying campaign.", "Fisher College announced on April 8 that one of their players would not be able to make it to PGL Astana due to a scheduling conflict, which later Dust2.us"], "news_summary": "B8 and Natus Vincere are set to face off in a Best-of-3 match at IEM Rio 2026 Group B, a $1M tournament in Rio de Janeiro from April 13-19, 2026. Natus Vincere is ranked #2 globally with a strong lineup including Aleksib, iM, b1t, w0nderful, and makazze, while B8 is ranked #18 and competing at a significant skill disadvantage. Recent stats show B8 posting a 1.11 rating with 71.4% KAST over the past 3 months, while Na'Vi demonstrates solid fundamentals as a top-tier team. This represents a matchup between a tier-1 powerhouse and a lower-ranked Ukrainian team.", "side_yes_case": ["Natus Vincere is ranked #2 globally with elite player caliber across all positions", "Na'Vi's recent stats show 1.16 rating and 74.5% KAST, indicating strong fundamentals and consistency", "Significant ranking gap (B8 #18 vs Na'Vi #2) suggests substantial competitive disparity", "Na'Vi has proven LAN experience at major tournaments versus B8's lower tier status"], "side_no_case": ["B8 is an underdog team ranked #18, significantly lower than Na'Vi's #2 ranking", "B8's stats (1.11 rating, 71.4% KAST) lag behind Na'Vi's metrics, indicating weaker performance", "B8 lacks the proven track record and roster stability of an established tier-1 organization", "Head-to-head historical data favors established tier-1 teams in matchups against lower-ranked challengers"], "key_context": ["IEM Rio 2026 is a premier $1M tournament with 16 teams competing April 13-19, 2026 in Brazil", "Match format is Best-of-3 in Group B stage, requiring consistency across multiple maps", "B8 and Na'Vi are both Ukrainian/Eastern European teams with regional rivalry history", "Na'Vi's lineup includes elite players like ZywOo's peers (Aleksib in-game leading) providing experience advantage", "Tournament location in Brazil may affect travel conditions and preparation time for Eastern European teams"], "data_freshness": "2026-04-13T10:19:36.331592Z", "price_history": {"yes": {"token_id": "19468732107850826124910683854873553648656766893335941507216915527138203216208", "data_points": 56, "weighted_avg"