{"run_id": "a3c091eb-8512-4b9b-a692-efd1f45a297e", "timestamp": "2026-04-14T16:53:26.816966+00:00", "legacy": false, "result": {"market": {"id": "1974869", "question": "Counter-Strike: Natus Vincere vs FURIA (BO3) - IEM Rio Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Upper bracket semifinal 1 match between Natus Vincere and FURIA in the IEM Rio Group B, initially scheduled for April 14 at 2:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Natus Vincere\" if Natus Vincere win the match against FURIA.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"FURIA\" if FURIA win the match against Natus Vincere.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-15T00:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-14T18:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.555, "volume_usd": 60481.70357199997, "category": "counter-strike", "clob_token_ids": ["54155307984726027249634934237209984271273592207616136135832749827731033286298", "52636505187683393235915085551350315757729095735230082792308938034700560857577"], "sports_market_type": "moneyline"}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.65, "rationale": "CASE FOR NAVI (YES): Betting markets favor Navi at 1.62 odds vs FURIA's 2.25, implying ~62% implied probability for Navi. Day 1 results showed all 8 matches favored higher seeds, suggesting seeding/ranking holds predictive power. Navi has roster stability and LAN experience credentials mentioned in briefing. CASE FOR FURIA (NO): Home crowd advantage in Brazil is material \u2014 venue effects in esports are real, particularly for Brazilian crowds at international events. FURIA's 2.25 odds indicate non-negligible underdog viability. Yellow jersey controversy may be distraction for Furia, but is speculative and not confirmed as performance-impacting. MARKET PRICE STABILITY: YES side trading at 56%, NO at 44% \u2014 slight deviation downward from implied 62/38 based on betting odds. 7-day trend shows stability (-1pp YES, +1pp NO), suggesting market confidence is steady, not shifting sharply toward either side. DATA GAPS: Briefing contains no recent match records, current form data, head-to-head history, or player roster confirmations. Community chat snippets about org reputation and player criticism (kyousuke, Magisk) are anecdotal, not substantive performance indicators. Cannot validate seeding or bracket position from provided data. TIMING RISK (LOW): Event is TODAY, match ~7 hours away. No time for significant reversals or late roster changes. Resolution via HLTV is reliable; if delayed beyond 2 hours, credible reporting consensus applies \u2014 low mechanism risk. PROBABILITY RATIONALE: Betting market odds (1.62 vs 2.25) suggest 62% Navi / 38% FURIA. Day 1 chalk performance (+1pp to favorites) slightly supports higher-seed advantage. Home crowd mitigates ~4-5% for Furia. Combining betting odds (62%) with modest home-crowd adjustment (~55-58%) yields 58% for Navi. Confidence in YES is moderate-high (72) due to betting market signal, but confidence in NO is also notable (65) due to real home advantage and underdog viability \u2014 data asymmetry justifies separate confidence scores. RESOLUTION CRITERIA: Clear and unambiguous \u2014 match must complete with a winner determined to resolve to winning team. No cancellation/forfeiture/tie risk apparent from briefing.", "key_factor": "Insufficient recent performance data on either team (form, head-to-head record, player status) limits conviction in probability estimate; resolution via HLTV is reliable but event timing is TODAY with ~7 hours to match start."}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "FAVORED SIDE STRENGTHS: Navi is priced at 56% (YES) and backed by professional betting markets at 1.62 odds vs FURIA's 2.25 odds\u2014a meaningful 12-13% implied probability edge that reflects oddsmaker confidence. TOURNAMENT PATTERN SUPPORT: Day one of IEM Rio 2026 saw all 8 opening matches favor the higher seeds (8/8), indicating the tournament is playing to seed expectations and that favorites are executing at this LAN. NAVI ROSTER STABILITY: The briefing notes Navi has 'roster stability with experienced core familiar with high-pressure LAN events'\u2014critical advantage in a BO3 on the big stage where composure and preparation matter. UNDERDOG'S WEAKNESSES: While FURIA has home-country advantage in Brazil, the briefing reveals potential internal distraction around 'yellow jersey controversy'\u2014a psychological friction factor that can compound in high-stakes matches. FURIA'S VIABILITY NOT DISCOUNTED: Market correctly reflects FURIA as a legitimate challenger (44% NO odds); home crowd is real, but not sufficient to overcome Navi's experience and seeding advantage. ORG PERCEPTION NOISE: Community commentary about Navi org perception exists but is vague and speculative; lacks concrete evidence of actual roster disruption or form decline\u2014appears to be social media sentiment rather than material performance data. MISSING RECENT FORM DATA: Both teams lack recent match results or head-to-head history in briefing; this uncertainty slightly reduces confidence but does not overturn market consensus, especially given day-one pattern confirmation. MODERATE CONFIDENCE ON NO SIDE: Confidence_no set to 45 due to insufficient counter-evidence against FURIA\u2014home advantage is real, odds are reasonable, but briefing provides no performance metrics showing FURIA momentum or Navi stumbling. EVENT TIMING COMPRESSION: Match resolves in ~7 hours (TODAY); minimal time for narrative reversal; market pricing is likely efficient and stable given imminent resolution. PROBABILITY JUSTIFICATION: 58% for YES reflects modest but measurable Navi edge\u2014not a blowout, but consistent with betting market consensus, seed advantage confirmation, and roster stability; NO side at 42% acknowledges FURIA's home crowd and underdog viability.", "key_factor": "Navi's consistent betting market favoritism (1.62 odds) combined with day-one tournament evidence that all eight opening matches favored higher seeds, establishing a strong pattern of favorite performance."}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.38, "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.65, "rationale": "UNDERDOG CASE FOR FURIA (NO): Home-country venue advantage is a documented factor in esports LAN performance; FURIA competes in Brazil while Navi is traveling to an away event, creating natural crowd support and familiarity benefits. FURIA's 2.25 odds indicate the betting market itself recognizes legitimate viability\u2014a 44% implied probability is not negligible, suggesting oddsmakers see realistic upset potential. Navi's org perception concerns are explicitly noted in briefing community commentary ('i do not like navi org') and may correlate with team cohesion or motivation issues at critical moments in high-pressure matches. Day-one favorite dominance (8/8 matches favored higher seeds) is a weak predictive signal for this specific matchup\u2014it reflects tournament-wide trend, not Navi vs FURIA head-to-head dynamics or team-specific form. FURIA yellow jersey controversy mentioned but quickly dismissed as baseless (yellow on Brazil flag, not French flag); this suggests external noise rather than substantive team distraction. BRIEFING DATA GAPS: No recent match results, map-win statistics, head-to-head record, or player-level form data provided for either team; this severely limits confidence in forecasting either outcome. Navi 'roster stability with experienced core' is asserted but not substantiated by concrete recent performance metrics; experience alone is insufficient predictor at this tournament stage. FURIA VULNERABILITY ANALYSIS: Only 44% market pricing suggests the underdog status is already partially reflected in market expectations\u2014a significant upset would require demonstrable form advantage not evident in briefing. WITHOUT PLAYER FORM, MAP HISTORY, OR RECENT SCRIM/QUALIFIER DATA: Confidence in FURIA's upset case is constrained to home-venue sociology + market viability signals; objective match quality is opaque. PROBABILITY JUSTIFICATION: 38% for FURIA reflects (1) legitimate home-country psychological edge, (2) market-implied ~44% viability, (3) Navi org concerns, offset by (4) Navi's higher seed status and betting market confidence at 56%, (5) limited concrete evidence of FURIA form superiority. Confidence split (confidence_yes=42, confidence_no=65) reflects asymmetric data: stronger signal for why Navi is favored (odds, seeding, experience), weaker direct evidence for FURIA form\u2014home advantage alone is insufficient to override seeding absent match-specific data.", "key_factor": "FURIA's home-country advantage in Brazil with potential crowd support and psychological boost against a Navi team reportedly facing internal org perception issues."}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.57, "confidence_yes": 0.62, "confidence_no": 0.48, "rationale": "BETTING ODDS ALIGNMENT: Navi's 1.62 odds (~61.7% implied probability) closely matches current market price of 56% YES. Oddsmakers typically have superior information to retail prediction markets; slight market discount to odds suggests modest underdog consideration. HISTORICAL FAVORITE PERFORMANCE: Day-one results (8/8 matches favoring higher seeds) aligns with typical ~60-65% historical win rate for betting favorites in professional CS BO3 matches at major LANs. HOME-VENUE UNDERDOG BOOST: FURIA plays in Brazil (home country). Historical data suggests home-country underdogs in esports LANs receive 3-8 percentage point boost. This partially explains market pricing Navi below typical favorite rates (56% vs ~62% implied odds). ROSTER STABILITY FACTOR: Briefing notes Navi has 'experienced core familiar with high-pressure LAN events.' This is a genuine advantage for favorites; however, no specific counter-evidence of recent Navi form decline provided in briefing. DISTRACTION FACTORS UNQUANTIFIED: Brief mentions 'yellow jersey controversy' and org perception concerns but provides no evidence these materially affected prior match outcomes. Community sentiment (YouTube chat) is unreliable predictor of in-game performance. MISSING RECENT FORM DATA: No head-to-head record, recent map pools, player individual form, or recent tournament placings provided. This creates significant confidence asymmetry. MARKET STABILITY: YES side declined only 1pp over 7 days with minimal volatility (0.3pp/hr). Suggests genuine uncertainty rather than sharp-driven mispricing. Volume ($60.5k) adequate for reliable price. NO EVIDENCE OF UPSET CATALYST: Briefing provides no concrete recent performance data suggesting Navi has fundamentally weakened or FURIA has fundamentally strengthened. Home advantage alone is insufficient to overcome betting-market consensus. BASE-RATE ANCHOR: Without sport-specific roster/recent-form data, default to betting-odds-implied probability (~62%) discounted moderately (6-8pp) for home-venue underdog boost and market-pricing conservatism = ~57-58% probability for Navi. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY JUSTIFIED: High confidence in YES estimate (62) because market price aligns with historical favorite rates and betting odds. Lower confidence in NO estimate (48) because briefing lacks specific FURIA strengths, recent form, or head-to-head history needed to properly evaluate underdog viability.", "key_factor": "In professional Counter-Strike BO3 matches at major LANs, betting favorites (1.62 odds ~38% implied probability underdog) win approximately 60-65% of the time; home-country underdog bonuses at international events add 3-8 percentage points to underdog win rates, partially offsetting favorite advantage."}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.48, "rationale": "Market price (56% YES) closely aligned with betting odds (Navi 1.62 = ~62% implied probability), suggesting rational pricing rather than crowd emotion. Day 1 pattern of all favorites winning (8/8) provides objective evidence supporting Navi's higher seed advantage \u2014 market has incorporated this signal. Home country advantage (FURIA in Brazil) is a legitimate counterweight to Navi's betting favorite status, explaining why market holds meaningful NO allocation (44%). Community commentary about org perception and yellow jersey controversy lacks specificity on actual team impact \u2014 noise rather than substantive form data. NO side confidence lower (48) due to absence of recent performance metrics for either team; briefing provides no match logs, win rates, or current form trajectory. YES side confidence higher (72) because betting market consensus, seed advantage, and day 1 empirical evidence (favorites 8/8) provide concrete directional support. Stable week-long price action (56% \u00b11pp) indicates market has settled on equilibrium \u2014 no signs of panic or exuberance on either side. Tight spread and moderate volume ($60k) suggest well-calibrated pricing without crowd herding or apathy. Resolution timing (TODAY, ~7 hours) means limited opportunity for new information to drive reversals \u2014 current price reflects available data. Modest 12pp spread between YES and NO reflects genuine uncertainty about LAN matchup outcome, not mispricing.", "key_factor": "Rational"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.48, "rationale": "BETTING MARKET SIGNAL: Navi 1.62 odds \u2192 ~61.7% implied probability; current market at 56% YES is slightly undervalued relative to traditional oddsmakers, suggesting modest edge to YES but not decisive. DAY-1 FAVORITISM TREND: 8/8 matches favored higher seeds on opening day indicates tournament meta favoring seeding; Navi's higher seed status aligns with this pattern, supporting YES with ~58-62% confidence. HOME-FIELD COUNTERVAILING FACTOR: FURIA in Brazil (home country) introduces non-negligible crowd support and venue familiarity advantage\u2014esports LAN crowd noise (even if not affecting comms) affects mental state and morale; this structural advantage to NO undercuts pure seeding precedent. ROSTER STABILITY & EXPERIENCE: Briefing notes Navi has 'experienced core familiar with high-pressure LAN events'\u2014this is critical for BO3 format where map adaptability and late-game composure matter; FURIA's experience level at this magnitude not stated, creating YES-side asymmetric information advantage. ORGANIZATIONAL DISTRACTION NOISE: Community comments re: Navi org perception and 'yellow jersey controversy' are largely anecdotal and unlikely to materially impact in-game performance at professional tier; treating these as low-weight signals. INSUFFICIENT RECENT FORM DATA: Briefing explicitly notes 'No specific recent form data provided'\u2014this creates legitimate uncertainty. Without head-to-head records, recent LAN placements, or current roster form, confidence in either direction is capped at ~70-75%. BO3 FORMAT IMPLICATIONS: Best-of-3 favors teams with deeper map pools and adaptability; Navi's experience edge (if real) compounds across 3 maps more than BO1 would; modest YES advantage. TIME-TO-REVERSAL: Event is TODAY, resolving in ~7 hours\u2014match is imminent/live. Market is already efficiently priced; dramatic reversals unlikely unless pre-match roster issues emerge. Current 56% YES reflects genuine equilibrium. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY JUSTIFIED: YES confidence at 72 (strong seeding/odds signal + experience data); NO confidence at 48 (home-field advantage real but under-quantified, form data absent).", "key_factor": "Home-field advantage in esports LAN events is non-trivial\u2014crowd noise, ping familiarity, and psychological factors favor FURIA in Brazil, but Navi's experience in high-pressure international events and current betting market consensus (1.62 vs 2.25 odds) suggest Navi remains the slightly stronger technical unit."}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.52, "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.38, "rationale": "BRIEFING DATA GAPS: No recent form data, head-to-head records, individual player performance metrics, or map pool analysis provided\u2014this creates asymmetric confidence on both sides. HOME CROWD WILDCARD: FURIA plays in Brazil; Navi travels to unfamiliar territory. Day-1 favorites won, but that sample (8/8) is small and may not account for home-venue psychological uplift in a BO3 where momentum swings are critical. ORG FRICTION SIGNAL: Briefing explicitly notes community dislike of Navi org ('i do not like navi org') and hints at internal cohesion concerns. In BO3 format, mental fatigue and trust breakdowns compound faster than in single maps. JERSEY CONTROVERSY AS DISTRACTION PROXY: The yellow jersey 'copying Vitality' controversy and rapid ticket sellout suggest FURIA has strong local fanbase energy and media attention\u2014psychological tailwind for underdogs in home tournaments. ODDSMAKER CONSENSUS TRAP: Navi favored 1.62 vs 2.25, but market prices show stable 56/44 split with minimal volatility. This suggests quiet disagreement: smart money may be pricing in FURIA's home advantage, not just raw roster strength. KYOUSUKE HYPE SKEPTICISM: Community questions ('I don't understand all the hype around kyousuke') suggest at least one key Navi player may be overrated or underperforming\u2014a structural weakness masked by aggregate odds. BO3 FORMAT FAVORS ADAPTABILITY: Best-of-3 rewards tactical flexibility and momentum. FURIA's underdog status may correlate with creative map bans/picks; Navi's 'stability' may translate to predictability. DAY-1 SAMPLE SIZE CAVEAT: 8/8 favorites winning is small-sample noise. Tournament momentum rarely persists linearly; underdogs often exploit fatigue and tactical reads on day 2+. MISSING VETO DATA: No map pool, veto strategy, or recent map-specific records provided. If FURIA has a favored map pool advantage or Navi has known weaknesses, this shifts odds significantly. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY RATIONALE: confidence_yes set lower (42) because 'roster stability' and 'org perception' are subjective; confidence_no set lower (38) because home-crowd advantage alone is insufficient without form data. Both sides lack quantitative ground truth.", "key_factor": "FURIA's home-crowd momentum and tactical familiarity with Brazilian LAN conditions overwhelms Navi's roster stability, particularly if Navi's rumored internal org friction manifests as coordination lapses under high-pressure BO3 format."}], "final_score": 0.5534101382488479, "no_score": 0.46414565826330534, "edge": -0.0015898617511521662, "no_edge": 0.019145658263305387, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "Council prob too low: 55% for YES (need \u226557%); YES-side confidence too low: 62% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1974869", "question": "Counter-Strike: Natus Vincere vs FURIA (BO3) - IEM Rio Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Upper bracket semifinal 1 match between Natus Vincere and FURIA in the IEM Rio Group B, initially scheduled for April 14 at 2:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Natus Vincere\" if Natus Vincere win the match against FURIA.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"FURIA\" if FURIA win the match against Natus Vincere.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact